{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:68d1f0499b5e7e300a78694452660b56d641726ccfba4c8fae3586127fddec1e","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.33},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT 1 (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE avg >=2.50%/yr, 2027-2036): measured marginal 52% vs an economic view of ~42%; the 10pp gap is pure index construction. Artefacts >3pp: (i) TURKIYE. OECD aggregates are PPP-GDP weighted and DO include Turkiye (~4% of OECD-38 PPP GDP). Turkiye core at 10-15% adds 0.40-0.60pp to the aggregate; even at 8% it adds ~0.25pp. On a 2.50% threshold this single inclusion is worth ~10-15pp of probability, upward. The OECD publishes an ex-Turkiye aggregate precisely because of this — which series resolves is UNSPECIFIED and is the largest single unresolved ambiguity in CQ2. (ii) \"core CPI/PCE\" mixes instruments. US core PCE runs persistently 25-35bp BELOW core CPI (weights, broader scope, chained formula). With the US ~37-40% of OECD-38 PPP GDP, the CPI-vs-PCE choice moves the aggregate 0.10-0.15pp = ~5-8pp of probability, upward if core CPI is used. (iii) Euro-area HICP excludes owner-occupied housing; the long-delayed phased integration of OOH into HICP (plausibly ~2029-2031, inside the window) would ADD to measured euro core mid-window, a one-way upward artefact worth 3-5pp. (iv) Core excludes food and energy, so the AI-buildout electricity channel reaches the measure only with a lag through services — a DOWNWARD artefact against the \"grid costs push inflation\" story, worth ~3pp. (v) Core series are essentially unrevised (index reconstitution aside), so a 10-year average is a clean arithmetic object — no revision risk, unlike GDP. 52%.\nCONJUNCT 2 (China headline CPI avg <1.50%/yr, 2027-2036): marginal 62%. Series = NBS monthly headline CPI. Resolution-friendly: NBS does not back-revise CPI. Artefacts: (i) basket weights are not fully published and are rebased every five years (2026 rebase due inside the window); pork weight alone has historically swung headline by 0.5-1.0pp. (ii) the series is widely assessed as SMOOTHED — a lower-variance series is mechanically more likely to remain inside a narrow band near its ~0.5-1.0% trend, which favours YES by maybe 4-6pp relative to a same-mean/higher-variance series. (iii) Rent/imputed housing is a small and methodologically contested weight, which has suppressed measured CPI through the property bust. Economically: 2023-26 ran 0.2-0.7%; Section 2A's 60M population contraction and 2.9T RMB pension subsidy burden lock in demand weakness; the Japan 1995-2015 reference class averaged ~0.0-0.3% over twenty years. The risk to NO is a decade-long policy reflation or an RMB-depreciation food/import shock. 62%.\nCORRELATION: near zero, with two offsetting channels. Positive: both legs are the same \"OECD bottleneck inflation / China deflation\" bifurcation narrative. Negative and mechanical: Chinese overcapacity EXPORTS goods disinflation to the OECD, which pushes OECD core down — but core is service-heavy, so the drag is weak. Net +0.10 effective. JOINT = 0.52 x 0.62 x 1.05 = 33.9%. 33.\nWINDOW WARNING: CQ2's OECD core window is 2027-2036 (10y) but the CQ6 lexical lock uses OECD core over 2032-2036 (5y). Reusing this number in CQ6 is a window error; I used ~+0.15pp for the back half (near-term capex-digestion disinflation, later care-wage and debt-service pressure).","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.316Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD switches its headline GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate to an ex-Turkiye bas